/**
 * SECTION:element-avsyncdetect
 * @see_also: avsynctestsrc
 *
 * Detects the flashes and tones generated by #GstAvSyncTestSrc.
 *
 * Video and audio pass through unchanged and the two streams never wait for each other, so
 * the element can be placed after any stage of a pipeline without adding latency. It only
 * accepts raw video and audio. In encoded parts of a pipeline, place it after a decoder.
 *
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:interval and #GstAvSyncDetect:frequencies must match the source.
 *
 * For each flash an `avsync-video-event` element message is posted with these fields:
 *
 * * `element` (string): name of this element, to tell several detectors apart.
 * * `index` (guint64): event index, read from the code rows of the frame. If they can't be
 *   read it is estimated from the stream time since the previous event.
 * * `frame-counter` (guint): frame counter from the code rows, only if they could be read.
 * * `pts` (GstClockTime): timestamp of the first frame of the flash.
 * * `running-time` (GstClockTime): running time of that frame, if it is inside the segment.
 * * `brightness` (gdouble): brightness of the frame between 0 and 1.
 * * `latency` (gint64): end-to-end latency of the frame, only if its clock time is encoded, see
 *   below.
 *
 * For each tone an `avsync-audio-event` element message is posted with these fields:
 *
 * * `element` (string): name of this element.
 * * `index` (guint64): event index. The frequency gives the index modulo the number of
 *   frequencies, the rest is estimated from the stream time since the previous tone, or since
 *   the start of the stream for the first one. If the pipeline restamps the stream it can be a
 *   multiple of the number of frequencies off until a tone is paired with a flash, which
 *   gives the full index.
 * * `frequency` (gdouble): frequency of the tone in Hz.
 * * `pts` (GstClockTime): timestamp of the first sample of the tone.
 * * `running-time` (GstClockTime): running time of that sample, if it is inside the segment.
 * * `level` (gdouble): peak amplitude of the tone, 1 is full scale.
 * * `noise-floor` (gdouble): level of the audio between tones, on the same scale.
 *
 * Audio messages are posted up to 15 ms of audio after the tone starts, once enough of it has
 * been seen to identify it. A tone must be louder than -40 dBFS and 12 dB above the noise
 * floor, which follows the level between tones within about a second.
 *
 * ## Pairing
 *
 * Each tone is paired with the flash of the same index, by running time. The offset is the
 * audio running time minus the video running time, so it is **positive when audio is late**
 * and negative when audio is early. Running times are what sinks synchronise to, so this is
 * the offset a viewer would see if the streams were rendered at this point of the pipeline.
 *
 * Tones only carry their index modulo the number of frequencies, so offsets up to half of
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:interval times the number of frequencies are measured correctly, 10 seconds
 * with the defaults. Larger offsets pair with the wrong event and appear that much smaller.
 *
 * For each pair an `avsync-pair` element message is posted with these fields:
 *
 * * `element` (string): name of this element.
 * * `index` (guint64): event index, from the flash.
 * * `video-running-time` (GstClockTime): running time of the flash.
 * * `audio-running-time` (GstClockTime): running time of the tone.
 * * `offset` (gint64): audio running time minus video running time, in nanoseconds.
 * * `drift` (gdouble): change of the offset over the last #GstAvSyncDetect:window pairs, in
 *   nanoseconds per second. Not set for the first pair.
 * * `within-tolerance` (gboolean): whether the offset is within #GstAvSyncDetect:tolerance-early
 *   and #GstAvSyncDetect:tolerance-late.
 * * `video-latency` (gint64), `audio-latency` (gint64): end-to-end latency of the flash and of the
 *   tone, only if the clock time of the flash is encoded.
 *
 * When the offset goes outside the tolerance a warning message is posted, once until it is back
 * inside. The default tolerances are the EBU R37 limits for a single stage, audio up to 40 ms
 * early and 60 ms late.
 *
 * Flashes without a tone count as #GstAvSyncDetect:missed-audio and tones without a flash as
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:missed-video, once the other stream has gone past the time they could have
 * been paired or has ended. Nothing is counted while only one stream is flowing.
 *
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:frequencies must be at least 200 Hz apart, as a tone is identified from its
 * first 10 ms.
 *
 * ## Clock rates
 *
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:video-clock-ppm and #GstAvSyncDetect:audio-clock-ppm compare the running
 * times of each stream's events with the times the source generated them at, over the last
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:window events. A positive value means the timestamps advance faster than the
 * pattern, for example because they come from a clock that runs fast compared to the clock the
 * pattern was played out with. They are measured per stream, so this also works with only one
 * stream linked. Without video, audio is compared against whole multiples of the interval, which
 * is up to a frame off unless the interval is a whole number of frames.
 *
 * ## Dropped and duplicated frames
 *
 * The frame counter is read from every frame. When it advances by more than the number of
 * frames that arrived, the missing frames count as #GstAvSyncDetect:frames-dropped, and when it
 * advances by less, the extra frames count as #GstAvSyncDetect:frames-duplicated. Frames whose
 * counter can't be read are included in the next frame that can be read, so a drop and a
 * duplicate in between cancel out. Counting starts again after a flush, a new segment or a gap
 * event, and when the counter goes backwards.
 *
 * For each frame after dropped or duplicated frames an `avsync-frame-error` element message is
 * posted with these fields:
 *
 * * `element` (string): name of this element.
 * * `frame-counter` (guint): frame counter of the frame.
 * * `pts` (GstClockTime): timestamp of the frame.
 * * `running-time` (GstClockTime): running time of the frame, if it is inside the segment.
 * * `dropped` (guint64): number of frames missing before it.
 * * `duplicated` (guint64): number of repeated frames before it.
 *
 * ## Lateness
 *
 * In PLAYING, the running time of the clock when each buffer arrives is compared with the
 * running time of the buffer. #GstAvSyncDetect:video-lateness and
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:audio-lateness are the difference for the last buffer of each stream, and
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:max-video-lateness and #GstAvSyncDetect:max-audio-lateness the largest so
 * far. A positive value means the buffer arrived after its running time. Sinks render a buffer
 * at its running time plus the pipeline latency, so a stream whose lateness exceeds the latency
 * is dropped or rendered late, and a difference between the streams shows how much headroom
 * each has at this point of the pipeline. This is only meaningful when the pipeline is
 * synchronised to the clock, for example with a live source or sinks that sync.
 *
 * ## End-to-end latency
 *
 * When #GstAvSyncTestSrc:encode-clock-time is set on the source, every frame carries the clock
 * time it was to be rendered at in the source's pipeline. In PLAYING, the detector subtracts it
 * from the clock time of the frame here, its running time plus the base time. This gives the
 * latency from the source to this point when a capture element in between restamps the stream,
 * for example in a loop from an output card to a capture card. Both pipelines must use the same
 * clock. Latencies up to about 35 minutes are measured correctly.
 *
 * Inside the pipeline that generates the pattern, or wherever timestamps are preserved, the clock
 * time of a frame is the same at the source and at the detector, so this latency is zero however
 * long processing takes. Use the lateness above to see how far behind its timestamps a stream is
 * within a pipeline.
 *
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:video-latency is the latency of the last frame, and
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:avg-video-latency and #GstAvSyncDetect:max-video-latency the average and
 * largest so far. Audio does not carry the clock time, its latency is the latency of the paired
 * flash plus the offset, in #GstAvSyncDetect:audio-latency, #GstAvSyncDetect:avg-audio-latency and
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:max-audio-latency.
 *
 * This is the latency up to the running time of the frame here. A sink after the detector renders
 * it #GstAvSyncDetect:pipeline-latency later.
 *
 * ## Reported latency
 *
 * The latency query is passed through unchanged. On the way, the detector records the latency
 * reported upstream of each sink pad in #GstAvSyncDetect:video-upstream-latency and
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:audio-upstream-latency, and the latency the pipeline then configures in
 * #GstAvSyncDetect:pipeline-latency. Buffers should never be later than the latency reported
 * upstream, so a lateness above it means an element upstream reports too little latency.
 *
 * When the pipeline configures its latency, an `avsync-latency` element message is posted for
 * each stream with these fields:
 *
 * * `element` (string): name of this element.
 * * `stream` (string): `video` or `audio`.
 * * `pipeline-latency` (GstClockTime): latency configured on the pipeline.
 * * `live` (gboolean): whether the stream is live upstream, if the query passed this element.
 * * `upstream-min-latency` (GstClockTime): minimum latency reported upstream, if the query passed
 *   this element.
 * * `upstream-max-latency` (GstClockTime): maximum latency reported upstream, if the query passed
 *   this element and it is not unlimited.
 *
 * ## Summary
 *
 * Once every stream that received caps is at EOS, an `avsync-summary` element message is
 * posted with `element`, `matched-events`, `missed-audio`, `missed-video`, `frames-dropped` and
 * `frames-duplicated`, and when known `last-offset`, `avg-offset`, `min-offset`, `max-offset`,
 * `drift`, `audio-clock-ppm`, `video-clock-ppm`, `max-audio-lateness`, `max-video-lateness`,
 * `avg-audio-latency`, `avg-video-latency`, `max-audio-latency`, `max-video-latency`,
 * `pipeline-latency`, `audio-upstream-latency` and `video-upstream-latency`, with the same meaning
 * as the properties. The properties keep their values after stopping, until the element is
 * started again.
 *
 * ## Example pipeline
 * |[
 * gst-launch-1.0 -m avsynctestsrc name=src avsyncdetect name=detect \
 *     src.video ! detect.video_sink detect.video_src ! fakesink \
 *     src.audio ! detect.audio_sink detect.audio_src ! fakesink
 * ]|
 *
 * Since: plugins-rs-0.16.0
 */
use gst::glib;
use gst::prelude::*;

mod imp;

glib::wrapper! {
    pub struct AvSyncDetect(ObjectSubclass<imp::AvSyncDetect>) @extends gst::Element, gst::Object;
}

pub fn register(plugin: &gst::Plugin) -> Result<(), glib::BoolError> {
    gst::Element::register(
        Some(plugin),
        "avsyncdetect",
        gst::Rank::NONE,
        AvSyncDetect::static_type(),
    )
}
